nvgpu: gm20b: register gpcclk without a device

The GPU's first power-on runs inside its runtime resume and prepares the GPU
clock, whose parents include nvgpu's own gpcclk. nvgpu registers gpcclk
with the GPU device, and since Linux 4.15 the clock framework
runtime-resumes a clock's device before preparing it. The GPU is still
resuming, so that call waits for itself while it holds the clock
framework's global prepare lock. Every other driver that touches a clock
then blocks too, the SD card host among them, and the boot stops.

Register gpcclk without a device. R32 registers it with the device, but
4.9's clock framework has no runtime PM, so this is how R32 behaves.

Found: trial 6 (SD card I/O stopped at the GPU's first power-on), traced in
trial 7 with diagnostic/0006.
Tested on the Nano: in trial 8 the clock step returned 0 and the power-on
completed.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
---
diff --git a/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c b/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
index a04276b76..f7da25486 100644
--- a/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
+++ b/nvidia/nvgpu/drivers/gpu/nvgpu/os/linux/platform_gk20a_tegra.c
@@ -848,7 +848,15 @@ static int gm20b_register_gpcclk(struct gk20a *g)
 
 	/* Data in .init is copied by clk_register(), so stack variable OK */
 	clk->hw.init = &init;
-	c = clk_register(dev_from_gk20a(g), &clk->hw);
+	/*
+	 * Register without the GPU device. Since Linux 4.15 the clock core
+	 * runtime-resumes a clock's device before preparing or re-rating it,
+	 * and gpcclk is first prepared from the GPU's own runtime resume
+	 * (gm20b_init_clk_support), which then waits on itself forever while
+	 * holding the clock framework's prepare lock. R32's 4.9 kernel has no
+	 * runtime PM in the clock core, so this matches how R32 behaves.
+	 */
+	c = clk_register(NULL, &clk->hw);
 	if (IS_ERR(c)) {
 		nvgpu_err(g, "Failed to register GPCPLL clock");
 		return -EINVAL;
